  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks to find a step function h on [0,2] that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. The current parent solution achieves C\u22480.3808, and our goal is to find a lower C value. The approach combines global exploration with local optimization while addressing the non-convex nature of the problem. We maintain several independent optimization processes with adaptive step sizes and priorities to avoid local optima. \n\n   Three main strategies:\n   - Parallel Perturbation and Refinement: Constant random mutations from the parent solution refined by coordinate descent\n   - Dynamic Neighborhood Exploration: Sampled regions identified by key features of the parent solution\n   - Hierarchical Budget Allocation: Prioritizes rapid descent followed by thorough refinement\n\n   Data-adaptive adjustments to sigma and iteration counts prevent premature convergence while valuing solutions with lower C.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   h \u2208 [0,1]^n   (n samples over [0,2])\n   \u2211h_i * (2/n) = 1   (integral constraint)\n   Objective: min max_{k\u2208[2n-1]} (\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx)\n\n   Computed via: max_k( np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')[k] ) * (2/n)\n\n   Constraints enforced by projection to [0,1]^n and normalizing sum to n/2.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Validate parent solution at n=700\n   - Initialize:\n        * sigma = 0.05\n        * best_c = computed value\n        * parallel_sols = [deep copy of parent]\n        * neighborhood_sols = []\n        * structure_sols = [deep copy of parent with key features preserved]\n\n   - Main loop (4 phases):\n      Phase 1 (first 10% budget): Greedy search with large sigma\n      Phase 2 (next 30%): Exploring regions guided by correlation matrix\n      Phase 3 (next 30%): Intensive refinement with larger population\n      Phase 4 (last 30%): Final hillclimbing on best feature set\n\n   - For each solution, apply:\n        * Projection: flatten_saturation(pro)\n        * Mutation: random perturbation \u223cN(0,\u03c3\u00b2)\n        * Refined by: exact_cd() with time deadline\n        * Evaluate: compute_c5 + feasibility check\n\n   - Budget management:\n        * Time tracking by phase\n        * Choose next action based on:\n            (a) Remaining budget\n            (b) Current improvement rate\n            (c) Diversity metrics \n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Default weights: \n        \u03c3_start = 0.05\n        \u03c3_decay_rate = 0.99\n        priority_weights = [0.5, 0.3, 0.1] per improvement per phase\n\n   Key variations:\n        Option A: Decrease \u03c3 by 0.95 for parallel population\n        Option B: Use structure-preserving mutation\n        Option C: L1 projection variant \n\n   Trigger conditions:\n        \u2013 Time milestones at 30%, 60%, 90% of budget\n        \u2013 Major improvement stagnation (no improvement for 50 steps)\n        \u2013 Constraint violation detection (project fails)\n\n5. Budget and Robustness:\n   Phase duration: 1/4 * budget_s seconds\n   Time checks: Verify deadlines inside exact_cd calls\n   Recovery: For compute errors, use fallback evaluation\n   Failure modes:\n        \u2013 Infeasible solution: \u03c3 reduction + fallback\n        \u2013 Time crunch: Jump to nearest solution\n        \u2013 Objective overflow: Clip convergence criteria\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Check: 0 <= h[i] <=1 + small tolerance\n   - Check: sum(h) = n/2 + small tolerance\n   - Check: finite c5 value\n   - Final candidate: Project and re-score exact_c5\n   - Output format: (h_array, c5, n)",
